<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sundiata Keita</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. c. 1255</span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> · Also known as Sundiata</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Sundiata <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/keita-dynasty">Keita</a> was the founder of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mali-empire">Mali Empire</a>, who died c. 1255. Around 1235 he defeated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sumanguru-kante">Sumanguru Kanté</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sosso-empire">Sosso</a> at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-kirina">Battle of Kirina</a>. Accounts of the Mali Empire credit him with uniting twelve kingdoms and establishing the empire's governance.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Sundiata is known chiefly through the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/epic-of-sundiata">Epic of Sundiata</a>, a semi-historical poem handed down orally by Maninka griots. According to it, he was the son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maghan-kon-fatta">Naré Maghann Konaté</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sogolon-conde">Sogolon Condé</a>, and he was crippled from childhood until he rose and walked. His half-brother Dankaran Touman and Dankaran's mother, Sassouma Bereté, resented him, and after his father's death Sogolon took her children into exile. They were eventually given asylum in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mema">Mema</a>, where its king gave Sundiata a senior position.</p><p>When King Soumaoro Kanté of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sosso-empire">Sosso</a> conquered the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mandinka">Mandinka</a>, messengers found Sundiata in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mema">Mema</a> and persuaded him to return. The epic says the king of Mema gave him an army, and that on the plain of Siby he and his allies, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tiramakhan-traore">Tiramakhan Traore</a>, formed a pact of brotherhood. Written accounts by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibn-battuta">Ibn Battuta</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibn-khaldun">Ibn Khaldun</a>, who travelled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mali">Mali</a> in the following century, give independent evidence that he existed.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>At the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-kirina">Battle of Kirina</a>, about 1235, Sundiata and his allies defeated Soumaoro, and he became the first emperor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mali">Mali</a> and the first <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mandinka">Mandinka</a> king to take the title <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mansa">Mansa</a>. He set his capital at Niani, near the present border of Mali with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guinea">Guinea</a>. His generals extended his power: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tiramakhan-traore">Tiramakhan Traore</a> is credited with conquests in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/senegambia">Senegambia</a>, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kaabu-empire">Kaabu</a>, and with defeating the king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jolof-empire">Jolof</a>, while Sundiata himself is credited with Diafunu and Kita.</p><p>Mansa Sundiata was not an absolute monarch. The empire was reportedly run like a federation, with each clan holding a chief representative at court, and the Great Gbara Assembly checked the Mansa's power and chose his successor. The Manden Charter and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kouroukan-fouga">Kouroukan Fouga</a>, unwritten and altered in retelling, are traced to his reign. Under him <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mali">Mali</a> began to control trade routes and gold fields, and he is reported to have introduced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotton">cotton</a> and weaving.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>The generally accepted year of his death is c. 1255. The cause is uncertain: the version now generally accepted is drowning in the Sankarani River, where a shrine bearing his name remains, while Maurice Delafosse reported an arrow during a ceremony and others an assassination. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mandinka">Mandinka</a> tradition forbids disclosing the burial ground of great kings. His sons Mansa Wali Keita, Mansa Ouati Keita and Mansa Khalifa Keita succeeded him, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mansa-musa">Mansa Musa</a> was his great-nephew. Scholars disagree on whether he was Muslim: the original epic does not say so, and later Muslim griots added Islamic ancestry to it.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Sundiata Keita” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After <b>Sundiata Keita</b> defeated Sumanguru at Kirina around 1235, the remnant of Ghana was incorporated into the Mali Empire, its capital traditionally said to have been abandoned around 1240.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ghana-empire">Ghana Empire · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Its origins lie in Mandinka expansion under <b>Sundiata Keita</b>, and oral tradition credits its founding to his general Tiramakhan Traore, whose conquests established frontier provinces governed by loyal military elites.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kaabu-empire">Kaabu Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Its foundation is attributed to <b>Sundiata Keita</b>, a prince of the Keita clan who, around 1235, formed a coalition of Mema, Wagadou and Mandinka forces to confront Sumanguru Kanté of the Sosso kingdom at the Battle of Kirina.</blockquote><a href="/empire/mali-empire">Mali Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The Mali Empire, founded by <b>Sundiata Keita</b> around 1235, ranked people above all by birth into a lineage.</blockquote><a href="/societies/mali-empire">Social Hierarchy of the Mali Empire</a></li><li><blockquote>The Epic of Sundiata is an epic poem of the Malinke people about <b>Sundiata Keita</b>, the founder of the Mali Empire in West Africa, who ruled from 1235 until his death in 1255.</blockquote><a href="/songs/epic-of-sundiata">Epic of Sundiata</a></li><li><blockquote>Tradition names Maghan Kon Fatta as the father of <b>Sundiata</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-kangaba">Kingdom of Kangaba</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Niani village, in what is now <a href="/countries/mali">Mali</a>. Died in Sankarani River, in what is now <a href="/countries/mali">Mali</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=11.38,-8.38&amp;year=1245" aria-label="Niani village on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4319 1739 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Sundiata Keita"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4769 2009h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4769 2009h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=11.38,-8.38&amp;year=1245">What was here in 1245? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
